// Green-thread scheduling core: the context switch, a task's saved register
// context, and the two run queues a worker pool draws from.
//
// Stage-2 port of `runtime/sched/` (#1364). Both exist during the port;
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE CONTEXT SWITCH IS SHAPED DIFFERENTLY HERE, AND THE REASON IS FORCED
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/sched/`'s switch saves only `sp`/`fp`/`pc` and declares every other GPR
// CLOBBERED, letting LLVM spill and reload the callee-saved registers around
// the block. It can do that because it is an `inline fn`: the asm lands in the
// caller's body, so the caller's allocator sees the clobber and spills to the
// caller's own frame — which `sp` restores on resume.
//
// Bit's `asm` has different semantics, stated in SPEC §11.6: a register named
// by `input`/`result`/`clobber` is "excluded from the register allocator for
// the whole enclosing function". EXCLUDED, not spilled. So a clobber list here
// does not cause the enclosing function to save anything — it merely stops that
// function from using the register, which means the CALLER's live value sits
// in it untouched and the switch would hand it to the resumed side. Clobbering
// is therefore not a substitute for saving, the way it is under LLVM.
//
// Nor can this be an inlined switch: Bit has no inline functions, and a
// per-call-site copy of a hand-encoded block is exactly the duplication that
// makes a hand-encoded block dangerous.
//
// So `schedSwitch` saves and restores the callee-saved file EXPLICITLY, which
// is what every standalone (non-compiler-assisted) context switch does —
// Boost.Context, Go's `gogo`, musl's `swapcontext`. The register sets are taken
// from this compiler's own backends, not from the ABI documents, because it is
// this compiler's allocation that must survive:
//
//   arm64  x19..x28 (`isCalleeSavedInt`), d8..d15 (`isCalleeSavedFloat`),
//          x29 (frame), x30 (link), sp
//   x64    rbx, r12..r15, rbp (frame), rsp; the return address lives on the
//          stack, so there is no link register to save
//
// See compiler/arm64compile.bit `isCalleeSavedInt`/`isCalleeSavedFloat` and
// compiler/x64value.bit's equivalents.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE x18 TRAP, AND WHY IT DOES NOT REACH THIS PORT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/sched/` must clobber `x18` on Linux and must NOT on Darwin. AArch64 calls
// x18 the platform register: Darwin reserves it, Linux leaves it
// general-purpose, so LLVM will park a live value there across the block on
// Linux only. A task that parks on one worker and resumes on another whose x18
// differs then faults on the first use — invisible on macOS, corrupting on
// Linux. That bug has already cost this project once.
//
// It cannot arise here, and the reason is structural rather than lucky: this
// compiler's arm64 backend never allocates x18 at all. `master_gprs`
// (compiler/arm64compile.bit) is x0..x8, x12..x15, x19..x28 — x18 is absent from
// the allocatable file on every target, so no Bit value can ever live in it,
// so there is nothing for a switch to preserve or destroy. An LLVM-based
// backend needs a clobber here because its allocatable set includes x18; this
// backend's does not.
//
// This is an argument from the backend's register file, so it is only as good
// as that file. If `master_gprs` ever gains x18, this switch must gain a save
// slot for it in the same change. The comment is the guard; the register list
// above is the thing to re-read.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DELIBERATE DEVIATIONS FROM THE USUAL SHAPE, ALL DISCLOSED
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// 1. Callee-saved registers are saved explicitly rather than via clobbers.
//    Forced by §11.6's exclusion semantics (above). Behaviourally identical:
//    both preserve the caller's live state across the switch.
//
// 2. d8..d15 ARE preserved here; `runtime/sched/`'s clobber list names no FP
//    register at all. That is a latent hazard in the original — this backend
//    allocates d8..d15 as callee-saved (`isCalleeSavedFloat`), so a task
//    holding a live float across a park would have it destroyed. Preserving
//    strictly MORE state than the original cannot change queue discipline,
//    ordering, or liveness; it only removes a corruption. Called out because
//    "behavioural equivalence" is the rule and this is the one place this port
//    is a superset.
//
//    THIS HALF IS NOT COVERED BY tests/stress/sched, and the gap is a
//    notational one rather than an oversight. The test pins register
//    preservation by writing sentinels into named physical registers and
//    reserving them against the allocator with a `clobber` list — but §11.6
//    admits only `x0`..`x30`, `sp` and `xzr` there, with no spelling for `d8`.
//    So an FP sentinel cannot be protected from the allocator, and a check
//    built on one would report the allocator's choices rather than the
//    switch's. Mutating the d8 slot away is confirmed NOT to fail the suite.
//    The 16 FP instructions are therefore carried on the strength of the
//    encoding review (disassembled and read back) alone, which is weaker
//    evidence than the rest of this file rests on. Treat them accordingly: the
//    integer file is proven, the FP file is argued.
//
// 3. Atomics are sequentially consistent throughout, because §11.5 exposes no
//    weaker ordering, where a scheduler would normally mix
//    acquire/release/relaxed. SC is strictly stronger than every ordering it
//    replaces, so every happens-before still holds.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MEMORY MODEL: EVERY STRUCTURE HERE IS CALLER-OWNED
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A task control block, a deque and a global queue are each a WORD-INDEXED
// BLOCK the caller supplies as a `*i64`, exactly as `runtime/alloc` and
// `runtime/gc` take their arenas. Nothing here allocates, and none of this
// memory may live on the GC heap: a `Task` holds a raw stack address with no
// `GcHeader`, so tracing it would decode arbitrary bytes as an object header
// (§11.11 makes the same argument for module state).
//
// It also keeps this file platform-free. Mapping a guarded stack is a syscall
// on Linux and a libSystem call on Darwin, and §11.7/§11.8 reject even an
// uncalled declaration of the wrong one, so the core cannot contain either.
// The consumer maps the memory and hands it over — the same handoff
// `gc/mem.bit` settled on.

import { spinAcquire, spinRelease, spinCellAt } from "../"
// `runtime/park`'s CORE only — it holds the backoff policy and no platform code,
// so importing it does not breach the rule that this file imports neither
// provider. `runtime/thread` depends on it the same way and for the same reason.
import { parkMinBackoffNs, parkNextBackoff } from "../park"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Saved register context
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Words in a saved context. The layout is shared by both architectures; each
// uses the prefix it needs, so one `Task` block shape compiles for either.
//
//   0        stack pointer
//   1        resume address
//   2        frame register   (x29 / rbp)
//   3        link register    (x30) / rbx on x64
//   4..13    x19..x28         (x64 uses 4..7 for r12..r15)
//   14..21   d8..d15          (arm64 only)
export const ctxWords: int = 22

export const ctxSp: int = 0
export const ctxPc: int = 1

const stackAlign: int = 16

// The entry-time stack-pointer bias, in bytes, for the architecture being
// compiled: 8 on x86-64, 0 on AArch64.
//
// The two disagree and there is no way to split the difference. x86-64 enters a
// function as though `call` had just pushed an 8-byte return address, so it
// requires `rsp % 16 == 8`; AAPCS64 requires `sp % 16 == 0` and enforces it in
// hardware — a misaligned `sp` faults on the first push. Biasing both alike
// SIGBUSes on arm64, which is exactly how this was found.
//
// The value is read out of a one-instruction `asm` block rather than from
// `hostTarget()` (§11.8) because `hostTarget` lowers to an out-of-line runtime
// call, which a `@nosplit` body may not make. A single immediate move is inline
// by construction, so it is legal in the nosplit callers below, and the
// distinction it draws is architectural rather than per-target: the same answer
// serves x86_64-linux, and both aarch64 targets share the other.
@nosplit fn entryBias(): int {
  return asm {
    arm64 { 0xD2800000 }                    // mov x0, #0
    x64   { 0xB8, 0x08, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00 }  // mov eax, 8
    result arm64 x0 x64 rax : int
  }
}

// Save the caller's callee-saved state into the context at `old`, restore the
// context at `new`, and resume there. Returns when some later switch names this
// context as its `new`.
//
// `volatile` is not spelled because §11.6 guarantees an `asm` block is never
// dropped, hoisted or deduplicated regardless.
//
// `@nosplit` (§10.3.1) for two reasons, both load-bearing. A safepoint must
// never land inside a half-saved context — the collector would read a task's
// registers from a slot the switch has not written yet. And a task body that is
// itself `@nosplit` (because the collector does not yet know its stack) can only
// call other `@nosplit` functions, so an ordinary switch would be unreachable
// from exactly the code that needs it. The block qualifies on the same proof as
// the atomics: it is a fixed inline sequence that neither allocates nor calls.
//
// The arm64 `adr` displacement (+184 bytes = 46 words) is the distance from the
// `adr` to the first word past `br`, hand-computed per §11.6 since there are no
// block-local labels. The x64 `lea` displacement (+64) is measured from the end
// of the `lea`. BOTH ARE POSITION-DEPENDENT ON THE INSTRUCTION COUNT: inserting
// or removing any instruction in either block invalidates them silently — the
// program branches into the middle of the block instead of resuming. There is
// no check for this; tests/stress/sched is what catches it.
export @nosplit fn schedSwitch(old: *i64, new: *i64) {
  asm {
    arm64 {
      0x910003E2,                                     // mov x2, sp
      0xF9000002,                                     // str x2, [x0, #0]
      0x100005C2,                                     // adr x2, +184 (resume)
      0xF9000402,                                     // str x2, [x0, #8]
      0xF900081D, 0xF9000C1E,                         // str x29, x30
      0xF9001013, 0xF9001414, 0xF9001815, 0xF9001C16, // str x19..x22
      0xF9002017, 0xF9002418, 0xF9002819, 0xF9002C1A, // str x23..x26
      0xF900301B, 0xF900341C,                         // str x27, x28
      0xFD003808, 0xFD003C09, 0xFD00400A, 0xFD00440B, // str d8..d11
      0xFD00480C, 0xFD004C0D, 0xFD00500E, 0xFD00540F, // str d12..d15
      0xF9400022,                                     // ldr x2, [x1, #0]   sp
      0xF9400423,                                     // ldr x3, [x1, #8]   pc
      0xF940083D, 0xF9400C3E,                         // ldr x29, x30
      0xF9401033, 0xF9401434, 0xF9401835, 0xF9401C36, // ldr x19..x22
      0xF9402037, 0xF9402438, 0xF9402839, 0xF9402C3A, // ldr x23..x26
      0xF940303B, 0xF940343C,                         // ldr x27, x28
      0xFD403828, 0xFD403C29, 0xFD40402A, 0xFD40442B, // ldr d8..d11
      0xFD40482C, 0xFD404C2D, 0xFD40502E, 0xFD40542F, // ldr d12..d15
      0x9100005F,                                     // mov sp, x2
      0xD61F0060                                      // br x3
    }
    x64 {
      0x48, 0x8D, 0x05, 0x40, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,       // lea rax, [rip+64]
      0x48, 0x89, 0x47, 0x08,                         // mov [rdi+8], rax
      0x48, 0x89, 0x27,                               // mov [rdi+0], rsp
      0x48, 0x89, 0x6F, 0x10,                         // mov [rdi+16], rbp
      0x48, 0x89, 0x5F, 0x18,                         // mov [rdi+24], rbx
      0x4C, 0x89, 0x67, 0x20,                         // mov [rdi+32], r12
      0x4C, 0x89, 0x6F, 0x28,                         // mov [rdi+40], r13
      0x4C, 0x89, 0x77, 0x30,                         // mov [rdi+48], r14
      0x4C, 0x89, 0x7F, 0x38,                         // mov [rdi+56], r15
      0x48, 0x8B, 0x26,                               // mov rsp, [rsi+0]
      0x48, 0x8B, 0x6E, 0x10,                         // mov rbp, [rsi+16]
      0x48, 0x8B, 0x5E, 0x18,                         // mov rbx, [rsi+24]
      0x4C, 0x8B, 0x66, 0x20,                         // mov r12, [rsi+32]
      0x4C, 0x8B, 0x6E, 0x28,                         // mov r13, [rsi+40]
      0x4C, 0x8B, 0x76, 0x30,                         // mov r14, [rsi+48]
      0x4C, 0x8B, 0x7E, 0x38,                         // mov r15, [rsi+56]
      0x48, 0x8B, 0x46, 0x08,                         // mov rax, [rsi+8]
      0xFF, 0xE0                                      // jmp rax
    }
    input arm64 x0 x64 rdi = old
    input arm64 x1 x64 rsi = new
    clobber arm64 { x2, x3, x19, x20, x21, x22, x23, x24, x25, x26, x27, x28, x30, memory }
    clobber x64 { rax, rbx, r12, r13, r14, r15, memory }
  }
}

// Round `addr` DOWN to the arch's entry-time stack alignment.
//
// Rounding down rather than up is deliberate: a stack grows downward from its
// top, so the aligned top must stay inside the mapping. Rounding up would place
// the first push above the last mapped byte — on a guarded mapping that is a
// fault on the first call the task makes, and on an unguarded one it is silent
// corruption of whatever follows.
@nosplit fn alignDown(addr: int): int {
  return addr - addr % stackAlign
}

// Build a fresh task's context at `ctx`, entering `entry` on a stack whose
// highest usable address is `stackTop`.
//
// `entry` comes from `entryOf(f)` (§11.10) — a named function's code entry.
// This is the call the whole task could not be constructed without, and the
// reason #1364 sat blocked: `int(f)` yields the closure OBJECT's heap address,
// which differs on every run, so a context built from it branches into the heap.
//
// The entered function is reached by a plain branch, never a call, so it takes
// no arguments and MUST NOT RETURN — there is no return address for it to
// return through (the link register is left 0 on arm64, and the x64 word at the
// initial `sp` is uninitialized stack). It recovers what to run from scheduler
// state and switches away when finished.
export @nosplit fn schedTaskContext(ctx: *i64, stackTop: int, entry: *byte) {
  *(ctx + ctxSp) = alignDown(stackTop) - entryBias()
  *(ctx + ctxPc) = int(entry)
  let i = 2
  while (i < ctxWords) {
    *(ctx + i) = 0
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// The lock word of a queue block, as the `*i32` `runtime/spinlock` requires.
//
// `spinlock.bit` fixed on i32 because §11.5 needs an integer prim and 32 bits
// is the conventional lock width. These blocks are word-indexed `*i64`, so the
// cell has to be re-addressed at its own width: a `*i32` scales by 4, so the
// byte address divided by 4 off the null pointer names the same cell. This is
// §11.4's stated inverse of pointer arithmetic, and the low half of the word is
// the one the spinlock touches on both targets (both are little-endian).
@nosplit fn lockOf(q: *i64, slot: int): *i32 {
  let z: *i32 = nil
  return z + int(q + slot) / 4
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Finding the current task without thread-local storage
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/sched/` reaches the running task through `threadlocal var tls: ?*Worker`.
// THAT MECHANISM IS NOT AVAILABLE TO THIS PORT, and the reason is not effort:
//
//   - `@threadlocal` (§11.11) is implemented on ELF but REJECTED FOR MACH-O at
//     emission, because a Mach-O TLV access calls a resolver thunk instead of
//     doing arithmetic off a thread pointer. So it cannot carry a Darwin worker.
//   - A `pthread_getspecific` shim is an `extern function`, and reading the
//     thread pointer out of `TPIDRRO_EL0` needs a platform seam too (Linux and
//     Darwin use different system registers on the same architecture). Either
//     way `@nosplit` REJECTS BOTH an `extern` call and a `syscall` (E0075,
//     measured), and every function here must be `@nosplit` — see the run
//     loop's comment for why that is forced rather than chosen.
//
// So the task pointer is carried by the one piece of per-thread state that
// needs no operating-system help at all: THE RUNNING TASK'S OWN STACK. Every
// task stack is `schedStackBytes` bytes, aligned to that size, and its lowest
// usable word holds the address of the task that owns it. Masking the live
// stack pointer down to that boundary therefore names the current task in two
// arithmetic instructions, on every target, inside `@nosplit`, with no TLS, no
// syscall and no platform split.
//
// It is also robust in a way a thread-local is not: it is a property of the
// stack rather than of the OS thread, so a task that migrates to another worker
// carries it along automatically and there is no per-switch bookkeeping to
// forget. `runtime/sched/` has to re-fetch `Worker.current()` after `fn_ptr` returns
// precisely because its answer changes under migration; this one cannot go
// stale.
//
// THE COST IS AN ALIGNMENT CONTRACT, and it is on the caller who maps the
// stack: `schedTaskInit`'s `stackBase` MUST be a multiple of `schedStackBytes`.
// Nothing can check that at compile time, so `schedStackAligned` is provided to
// check it at run time and the mapping code is expected to use it. A misaligned
// stack does not fault — it silently resolves to the wrong task, which is why
// the check exists rather than a comment asking for care.
//
// THIS IS THE IDENTITY GRANULE, and only incidentally a stack size (#1843).
// Identity is "mask sp down to this and read the tag at the lowest word", so a
// stack masked from anywhere inside it must land on ITS OWN base — which caps a
// masked-identity stack at exactly this size. The interior boundaries cannot
// carry copies of the tag: they fall inside the usable stack, where ordinary
// frames overwrite them.
//
// So a task wanting MORE than this cannot use masking, and `main` is exactly that
// task — see `schedRegisterBigStack` below, which is how it gets 8 MiB.
//
// 64KiB is unchanged, but the original justification for it was not: it read
// "64KiB is `runtime/sched/`'s `stack_size` unchanged", which was true when written
// and false by the time G3 landed. `runtime/sched/` carries `stack_size = 256 * 1024`
// and `main_stack_size = 8 * 1024 * 1024`. Matching the first means rescaling the
// twelve stress programs that carve `(n+1) * schedStackBytes` arenas out of fixed
// module arrays, so it is filed rather than done here; matching the second is
// what the oversized-stack registry does, and that is the one that was crashing.
//
// The bottom of the stack is not all usable: `scratchOf` puts the chan scratch
// area at words 0..`chanScratchWords` of this same base, words 0 and 1 being the
// self-pointer and tag. Usable stack starts above that.
export const schedStackBytes: int = 65536

// Written next to the self-pointer to mark a region as a TASK stack.
//
// Without it this scheme is unsound off a task, and not in a subtle way:
// `runtime/sched/`'s `Worker.tls` is simply null on a non-worker thread, but masking
// the stack pointer always yields SOME address, so a call from the boot thread
// or from a worker's own loop would read whatever that thread's stack happens to
// hold there and then dereference it as a task. `Scheduler.spawn` is documented
// callable from any thread, so that path is reachable by design rather than by
// misuse.
//
// Checking a tag makes the answer sound: both words live inside the caller's own
// live stack, so reading them is always safe, and a non-task stack matching a
// 64-bit constant at exactly the 64KiB boundary is not a case worth costing
// anything to exclude further. The value is arbitrary and deliberately not a
// plausible pointer, small integer, or ASCII run.
const schedStackTag: int = 0x5CED7A5C5CED7A5C

// Whether `base` satisfies the alignment contract above.
export @nosplit fn schedStackAligned(base: int): bool {
  return base % schedStackBytes == 0
}

// The live stack pointer. One instruction, so it is inline by construction and
// legal in a `@nosplit` body.
//
// Reading `sp` inside a callee rather than at the use site is deliberate and
// safe: a callee's prologue only ever moves `sp` DOWN within the same stack, and
// the mask below discards everything below the 64KiB boundary, so the answer is
// the same whichever frame asks.
@nosplit fn readSp(): int {
  return asm {
    arm64 { 0x910003E0 }              // mov x0, sp
    x64   { 0x48, 0x89, 0xE0 }        // mov rax, rsp
    result arm64 x0 x64 rax : int
  }
}

// The task running on this thread, or 0 when the caller is not on a task stack.
//
// The zero answer is what a worker's own loop gets, since a worker runs on the
// OS thread stack its `threadStart` mapped, not on a task stack. That word is
// whatever the thread stack happens to hold there, so callers must treat this
// as "ask only from task code" — which is exactly `runtime/sched/`'s contract for
// `Worker.current()` ("task code only ever runs on a worker OS thread").
// `let` then `return` rather than `return *(...)` throughout this file: a
// dereference is not accepted in return position today (E0021), only in `let`
// and assignment position. Filed on #1421; harmless, but it shapes every
// accessor here.
// The task owning the stack region based at `base`, or 0 if that region is not
// a task stack.
//
// Split out from `schedCurrentTask` so the tag check is REACHABLE BY A TEST.
// Driven only through `schedCurrentTask`, the check cannot be made to fail: a
// fresh thread stack reads 0 at its 64KiB boundary whether or not the tag is
// consulted, so a test asserting "0 off a task" passes even with the check
// deleted — measured, not assumed. Taking the base as a parameter lets a test
// point it at a region it has deliberately filled with garbage, which is the
// only way to tell a working check from an absent one.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_task_on_stack") fn schedTaskOnStack(base: int): int {
  let b = taskAt(base)
  let tag = *(b + 1)
  if (tag != schedStackTag) {
    return 0
  }
  let t = *b
  return t
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// OVERSIZED STACKS (#1843): the tasks masking cannot name
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `main` needs a stack far larger than the granule. `runtime/sched/` gives it
// `main_stack_size` = 8 MiB against a 256 KiB goroutine, because `main` is where
// user code recurses — #1761 was `bit fmt`'s recursive-descent formatter
// overflowing exactly this, and the port shipping `main` at 64KiB is what
// `tests/stress/deeprecursion` caught.
//
// Masking cannot describe such a stack (see `schedStackBytes`), and raising the
// granule to 8 MiB is not the answer either: the granule is the alignment unit,
// so every goroutine would reserve 16 MiB, and the twelve stress programs that
// carve `(n+1) * schedStackBytes` arenas out of fixed module arrays would all
// need rescaling.
//
// So oversized stacks get a REGISTRY instead, and the fast path never sees it:
// `schedCurrentTask` masks first, exactly as before, and only consults this when
// masking finds no tag. That miss already happened on every worker OS thread, so
// the cost is a few compares on a path that previously just returned 0.
//
// A FIXED FOUR ENTRIES, not a growable list, and the read loop is bounded by the
// constant rather than by a live count so its limit is statically provable. Four
// is `main` plus slack; a fifth registration is refused rather than silently
// dropped, since a task whose stack nothing can name would resolve to 0 and
// null-deref at its first `schedYield`.
//
// A slot freed by `schedUnregisterBigStack` becomes available to a later
// `schedRegisterBigStack` call — the table has no notion of "retired", only
// "occupied" and "free" — so a long-lived process that spawns and tears down
// oversized-stack tasks one at a time never exhausts the four slots.
export const schedBigStackMax: int = 4

// Three words per entry — base, bytes, task — and a zero base means empty.
let schedBigStacks: [12]i64

// How many of the four slots are occupied. BOOKKEEPING ONLY: the scan loops in
// `schedRegisterBigStack`/`schedTaskOnBigStack` stay bounded by the constant
// `schedBigStackMax` above, not by this count, for the same provability reason
// the comment above gives — a live count could in principle be read torn or
// stale across a race and would then no longer bound anything, whereas the
// constant needs no read at all. This exists so a caller (or a future
// diagnostic) can ask "how full is the table" without a linear scan.
//
// The `+ 1` (below) and `- 1` (`schedUnregisterBigStack`) are plain
// read-modify-writes, not atomics, and that is safe for the identical
// boot-order reason `schedRegisterBigStack`'s header gives (#3298): the
// increment runs on the process's one boot thread before any worker OS
// thread exists, and the decrement path is presently unreachable (zero
// callers anywhere in the tree). Re-derive both if a second caller of either
// function is ever added.
let schedBigStackCount: int = 0

// Record `task` as owning `[base, base + bytes)`. False if the table is full or
// the arguments are not a usable range, and the caller MUST treat that as fatal:
// an unregistered oversized stack is a task `schedCurrentTask` cannot name.
//
// Pinned: `runtime/root/{linux,darwin}/boot.bit` calls it across a module
// boundary and emits `--freestanding`, which refuses a mangled name.
//
// THE CLAIM LOOP BELOW IS A PLAIN LOAD-THEN-STORE, NOT A CAS, AND THAT IS
// SAFE BY BOOT ORDER, NOT BY LUCK (#3298, epic #3275). There are exactly two
// callers in the whole tree — `runtime/root/darwin/boot.bit:639` and
// `runtime/root/linux/boottail.bit:331` — mutually exclusive at build time
// (a binary links one platform's boot, never both), and each calls this
// exactly once, to register `main`'s own oversized stack, at step 5 of
// `boot`, strictly BEFORE step 6 spawns the first worker OS thread
// (`threadStart`). `boot` itself cannot run twice in one process (guarded by
// `root.bit`'s `booted` flag, asserted false on entry). So at every call that
// ever executes, this is the only thread that exists: no second writer can
// ever be mid-scan. This is `runtime/ABI.md` §22's
// write-once-before-any-worker-exists category — the same one `gArgc` and
// `booted` are in — not the single-worker contract and not adjacency (§5.1).
// `schedUnregisterBigStack` below has ZERO callers anywhere in the tree, so
// the free-slot-reuse path this scan exists for is presently unreachable
// too. A CAS here would guard a race that cannot happen, at the cost of a
// compare-exchange no caller needs — do not add one without a third caller
// first, and re-derive this whole comment if one is ever added.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_register_big_stack") fn schedRegisterBigStack(base: int, bytes: int, task: int): bool {
  if (base <= 0 || bytes <= 0 || task == 0) {
    return false
  }
  let tbl: *i64 = ptrOf(schedBigStacks)
  let i = 0
  // Bounded by the constant, and first-free wins.
  while (i < schedBigStackMax) {
    if (*(tbl + i * 3) == 0) {
      *(tbl + i * 3 + 1) = bytes
      *(tbl + i * 3 + 2) = task
      // Base LAST: it is the validity flag every reader tests, so publishing it
      // after the other two words means a reader can never see a live entry
      // whose length and task are not yet written.
      *(tbl + i * 3) = base
      schedBigStackCount = schedBigStackCount + 1
      return true
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return false
}

// Undo a `schedRegisterBigStack(base, ...)`, freeing its slot for reuse. A no-op
// when `base` is not currently registered — including `base <= 0`, which can
// never be a live entry — so a caller does not need to track whether it already
// unregistered.
//
// Bounded by `schedBigStackMax`, exactly as the register and lookup paths are.
export @nosplit fn schedUnregisterBigStack(base: int) {
  if (base <= 0) {
    return
  }
  let tbl: *i64 = ptrOf(schedBigStacks)
  let i = 0
  while (i < schedBigStackMax) {
    if (*(tbl + i * 3) == base) {
      // Base FIRST: it is the validity flag every reader tests (see
      // `schedRegisterBigStack`), so clearing it before the other two words
      // means a concurrent reader can never observe a half-cleared entry as
      // still valid — it either sees the old, fully-formed entry or an empty
      // slot, never a base-less task/bytes pair.
      *(tbl + i * 3) = 0
      *(tbl + i * 3 + 1) = 0
      *(tbl + i * 3 + 2) = 0
      schedBigStackCount = schedBigStackCount - 1
      return
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// The oversized-stack task containing `sp`, or 0. Bounded by `schedBigStackMax`.
@nosplit fn schedTaskOnBigStack(sp: int): int {
  let tbl: *i64 = ptrOf(schedBigStacks)
  let i = 0
  while (i < schedBigStackMax) {
    let base = *(tbl + i * 3)
    if (base != 0 && sp >= base && sp < base + *(tbl + i * 3 + 1)) {
      return *(tbl + i * 3 + 2)
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return 0
}

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_current_task") fn schedCurrentTask(): int {
  let sp = readSp()
  // `& ~(schedStackBytes - 1)` rather than `- sp % schedStackBytes` (#3318).
  // Identical for a power-of-two granule, which this one is and must be — the
  // whole scheme is "mask the stack pointer down to its base" (see
  // `schedStackBytes` above), so a granule that is not a power of two would
  // already have broken `schedTaskInit`'s alignment contract before reaching
  // here. A signed `%` by a divisor the backend loads into a register costs an
  // `sdiv` + `msub` plus a divide-by-zero trap branch, five instructions on the
  // single most-called lookup in the runtime, where the mask is one.
  let t = schedTaskOnStack(sp & ~(schedStackBytes - 1))
  if (t != 0) {
    return t
  }
  return schedTaskOnBigStack(sp)
}

// The worker currently running the calling task. `runtime/sched/`'s `Worker.current()`.
//
// PINNED (ABI.md §9, #2318): `runtime/root/{linux,darwin}/time.bit` reach this
// across a module boundary (to find a task's own `wkSleepReq` scratch for
// `parkMonoNs`/`clock_gettime`), and this body is an `if` rather than a single
// block, so the inliner does not fold it away — an unpinned export would be
// emitted as `m<N>$schedCurrentWorker`, a name no separately emitted object can
// define (SPEC §11.9), which is exactly the error this pin fixes.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_current_worker") fn schedCurrentWorker(): int {
  let task = schedCurrentTask()
  if (task == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let w = *(taskAt(task) + taskWorker)
  return w
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The idle backoff
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// DEVIATION, AND IT IS A REAL ONE: `runtime/sched/` SLEEPS between idle cycles
// (`sleepNs(backoff)`); this SPINS. The policy is unchanged — the same doubling
// from 1us to 1ms, taken from `runtime/park`'s `parkNextBackoff` so there is one
// copy of it — but the mechanism is a pause loop rather than `nanosleep`.
//
// It WAS forced rather than preferred: the run loop had to be `@nosplit`, and
// `@nosplit` rejects BOTH a `syscall` and an `extern` call with E0075 —
// measured, not inferred — while `parkSleepNs` is one or the other on every
// target. There was no third spelling; a call through a function value is also
// E0075.
//
// THAT `@nosplit` BLOCKER IS GONE (#1431), BUT A DIFFERENT ONE REPLACED IT, and
// the note above was wrong to call the swap "ordinary work" for THIS file: the
// sleep the run loop would call is `runtime/park/<os>`'s `parkSleepNs`, which is
// in a provider this file may not import and whose pinned symbol is not a member
// of `libbitrt.a`, so it is unreachable by `extern` as well (E0078, verified on
// both Linux triples). Only a caller that owns the `while` can sleep — see the
// note on `spinBackoff` below, and #1433.
//
// THE ITERATION COUNT IS NOW ONLY A FLOOR; THE CEILING IS THE MONOTONIC CLOCK
// (#1649).
//
// It used to be both, and that was a measured user-visible bug. The old body was
// the loop below WITHOUT the deadline check:
//
//     let iters = backoffNs / parkMinBackoffNs
//     while (i < iters) { <yield/pause> ; i = i + 1 }
//
// i.e. it converted a NANOSECOND budget into an iteration count by asserting
// that one `yield`/`pause` costs exactly 1us. Nothing makes that true, and it is
// wrong in BOTH directions: a `yield` on native AArch64 is a couple of
// nanoseconds, so a nominal 1ms wait elapsed in ~2us; an emulated `pause` is
// orders of magnitude slower (#1431 measured ~100x between the two), so the same
// nominal 1ms wait elapsed in ~11ms.
//
// Since #1648 the OVERSHOOT direction is user-visible as timer wake latency:
// this loop is what drives `schedTimerExpire`, so a worker that waits 11ms when
// it meant to wait 1ms expires timers up to 11ms late. Measured on #1649's
// `sleep(50ms)` repro under emulated x86_64 — this runtime returned 51..61ms
// where the previous one returned 50..51ms.
//
// SO THE DEADLINE IS A CEILING, NOT A TARGET, AND THAT ASYMMETRY IS DELIBERATE.
// Overshooting the budget costs LATENCY, which is the bug. Undershooting it
// costs only CPU — and this wait burns a whole core either way (see below), so
// undershooting costs nothing that is not already being spent. Making the loop
// spin UNTIL the deadline would therefore have been a pure regression on native
// AArch64: measured on this box, a `sleep(50ms)` on the boot-worker path returns
// in 50001us today and would return in up to 51000us. The clock is added as an
// early exit and the iteration count is left exactly as it was, so no host gets
// slower and the emulated host stops overshooting.
//
// Recalibrating the iteration count was explicitly not an option: a calibrated
// spin is calibrated for one host, and the next host (a loaded box, a different
// arch, a VM) invalidates it again. A wall-clock ceiling cannot go stale.
//
// WHY THIS IS STILL A SPIN AT ALL, AND WHAT IT COSTS. `runtime/sched/` SLEEPS here
// (`sleepNs(backoff)` bracketed by `bit_rt_gc_blocking_begin`/`_end`), and that
// remains the target. It is not reachable from this file: the OS sleep is
// `syscall` on Linux and `extern` on Darwin, §11.7/§11.8 reject even an uncalled
// declaration of the wrong one, and the providers' pinned
// `bit_rt_port_park_sleep_ns` is NOT a member of `libbitrt.a`, so an `extern`
// reference to it is refused by E0078 on both Linux triples (verified, not
// assumed). Only a caller that owns the `while` can sleep — `runtime/root/<os>`'s
// `workerBody`, which already imports `parkSleepNs`. That half is #1433's.
//
// CPU cost of what IS here, stated rather than implied: UNCHANGED. An idle
// worker burned a whole core before this change and burns a whole core after it
// — "N idle workers peg N cores" (worker.bit's consequence 2) still stands. The
// added work is one clock read per hint instruction, which is real but is spent
// on a core that was already spinning; on the hosts where the ceiling never
// trips the loop simply runs its old iteration count slightly slower.
//
// WHY NO `bit_rt_gc_blocking_begin`/`_end` BRACKET. The bracket exists for a
// thread that goes to sleep INSIDE A SYSCALL, where it is neither `running` nor
// `parked` and can answer no stop-the-world rendezvous (ABI.md §5). A spinning
// thread is an ordinary running mutator that reaches the run loop's back-edge
// safepoint as soon as this returns. The ceiling only ever SHORTENS that, so the
// contract this change has to keep is kept by construction. It is the
// caller-sleeps change above, not this one, that will need the bracket.
const IDLE_WAIT_MAX_POLLS: int = 1000

// The monotonic clock, reached as a RUNTIME-ARCHIVE SYMBOL rather than by an
// import, and that spelling is forced.
//
// This file may import neither park provider (see `schedFindWorkIdle`'s platform
// seam), and the seam it does have hands the clock in as a VALUE — no use to a
// loop that must re-read it. `bit_rt_time_mono_ns` is the one spelling that works
// on all three targets: it IS a member of `libbitrt.a` everywhere, so SPEC
// §11.7's E0078 admits it on the Linux triples too (#1444 narrowed that predicate
// to archive membership), and it is the SAME `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` reading that
// `runtime/park`'s `parkMonoNs` performs — so this loop and the deadlines in
// `schedTimerPush` are on one clock, which is the mismatch that was #1648.
//
// It resolves against a Bit-sourced archive as well: `runtime/root/<os>/time.bit`
// pins the same function under this bare name, since the G2 rename (#1583/#1584)
// dropped the `_root` infix — the same position `bit_rt_gc_blocking_begin` is
// in for `runtime/sched/`'s own idle path.
extern fn bit_rt_time_mono_ns(): i64

// `@nosplit` because `spinBackoff` is, and a `@nosplit` body may call an
// `extern function` but not a non-`@nosplit` Bit one (E0075, #1640) — which is
// why `bit_rt_time_mono_ns` is reached as an extern symbol above rather than
// through `runtime/park`'s `parkMonoNs`. `parkMonoNs` is `@nosplit` itself
// today (since #3000, `07cb7ae8`), so E0075 no longer forbids the call; the
// reason this file still can't make it is the platform seam above —
// `sched.bit` may import neither park provider, and `parkMonoNs` lives in one.
@nosplit fn monoNs(): int {
  return int(bit_rt_time_mono_ns())
}

// The iteration count tracks the nanosecond policy so the shape of the backoff
// is preserved: `backoff / parkMinBackoffNs` is 1 at the floor and 1000 at the
// cap. Both architectures' hint instructions are the ones their spin primitives
// use (`yield` on AArch64, `pause` on x86-64) — the same two `runtime/spinlock`
// already spins on.
//
// `iters` is clamped rather than trusted: `backoffNs` is the CALLER's and crosses
// a module boundary, and Power of 10 wants the bound provable from this body
// alone. `IDLE_WAIT_MAX_POLLS` is `parkMaxBackoffNs / parkMinBackoffNs`, so the
// clamp is unreachable for every backoff `parkNextBackoff` can produce.
@nosplit fn spinBackoff(backoffNs: int) {
  let iters = backoffNs / parkMinBackoffNs
  if (iters > IDLE_WAIT_MAX_POLLS) {
    iters = IDLE_WAIT_MAX_POLLS
  }
  let start = monoNs()
  let i = 0
  while (i < iters) {
    if (monoNs() - start >= backoffNs) {
      return
    }
    asm {
      arm64 { 0xD503203F }        // yield
      x64   { 0xF3, 0x90 }        // pause
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
}
